      "text": "### 1. Parry-Lord Oral-Formulaic Theory (Primary Framework)"
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#015",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:oral-tradition:00-overview, git-history:oral-tradition:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "oral-tradition/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "oral-tradition/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:oral-tradition:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "oral-tradition",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "oral-tradition",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Oral Tradition: Memory, Performance, and Transmission - Landscape",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nMILMAN PARRY (1928-1935)\n    |\n    Fieldwork: Slavic guslars in Yugoslavia → sang epic without memorizing\n    Dissertation: Homeric formulas are functional, not decorative\n    Key: metrical necessity explains formula repetition\n    |\n    v\nALBERT LORD (1960 — \"The Singer of Tales\")\n    |\n    Systematized Parry's findings\n    Formula: \"a group of words regularly employed\n              under the same metrical conditions\n              to express a given essential idea\"\n    Theme: \"groups of ideas regularly used in\n            telling a tale in the formulaic style\"\n    |\n    v\nJOHN MILES FOLEY (1980-2012)\n    Extended to Old English, South Slavic, Ancient Greek comparatively\n    \"Oral-derived text\" distinction (not all old texts are primary oral)\n    \"Immanent art\" — traditional referentiality (allusion without citation)\n```"
    },
