      "text": "### 2. Walter Ong — Orality and Literacy (1982)"
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#017",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:oral-tradition:00-overview, git-history:oral-tradition:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "oral-tradition/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "oral-tradition/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:oral-tradition:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "oral-tradition",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "oral-tradition",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Oral Tradition: Memory, Performance, and Transmission - Landscape",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Nine characteristics of primary oral thought and expression:\n1. **Additive** rather than subordinative (and...and...and)\n2. **Aggregative** rather than analytic (not \"soldier\" but \"brave soldier\")\n3. **Redundant** (repetition maintains thread without text reference)\n4. **Conservative** (tradition bearer preserves what is sanctioned)\n5. **Close to the human lifeworld** (abstract categories impractical without text)\n6. **Agonistically toned** (verbal combat as a knowledge form)\n7. **Empathetic and participatory** rather than distantly objective\n8. **Homeostatic** (present-focus, past irrelevancies drop away)\n9. **Situational** rather than abstract (thinking stays in context)"
    },
