        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Oral Tradition: Memory, Performance, and Transmission - Landscape",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "| Region | Tradition | Principal Form | Key Features |\n|--------|-----------|----------------|--------------|\n| Ancient Greece | Homeric epic | Hexameter | Formula-dense, rhapsodic performance |\n| South Asia | Sanskrit epic (Mbh, Ram) | Shloka couplet | Interpolation-heavy, 100,000 verses |\n| West Africa | Griot (jeli) tradition | Prose + song | Hereditary caste, political function |\n| North Europe | Norse skaldic | Strict kenning verse | Court context, kenning density |\n| Anglo-Saxon | Old English alliterative | Four-beat alliterative | Formula parallels Greek |\n| Near East | Mesopotamian cycle | Akkadian syllabic | Gilgamesh earliest surviving epic |\n| Finland | Kalevala | Trochaic tetrameter | Collected 1835 from folk singers |\n| Pacific | Polynesian genealogy | Chant | Mnemonic compression, thousands of names |\n| Americas | Indigenous oral law | Varied | Legal force, land rights function |\n| DRC (Nyanga) | Mwindo cycle | Prose epic | Performed over multiple nights |"
    },
