        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Oral Tradition: Memory, Performance, and Transmission - Landscape",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nPRIMARY ORAL CULTURE\n   No writing whatsoever\n   Thought shaped by formulaic patterns\n   Knowledge = what can be remembered\n   Example: pre-literate Slavic guslars\n        |\n        v (writing introduced)\nTRANSITIONAL / RESIDUAL ORALITY\n   Writing exists but not widespread\n   Oral composition habits persist in written texts\n   \"Oral-derived texts\" — Homer, Beowulf, medieval chansons\n   Formula density: 30-40% repeated phrases\n        |\n        v (literacy expands)\nHIGH LITERACY\n   Text is assumed primary medium\n   Oral residue in folklore, gossip, rumor\n   \"Secondary orality\" (Ong): radio, TV, internet\n   New oral genres: podcast, talk radio, voicemail\n        |\n        v (networked digital)\nSECONDARY ORALITY / DIGITAL ORALITY\n   Text-based media with oral characteristics\n   Participatory, formulaic (memes), agonistic (Twitter)\n   Internet meme as oral formula analog\n```"
    },
