      },
      "text": "| Question | Answer |\n|----------|--------|\n| Is Homer one poet or many? | Oral-formulaic theory makes the question wrong — each performance is a re-composition |\n| Is an oral tradition accurate? | Stable structure, variable surface — facts drift, schema persists |\n| Can oral cultures have complex thought? | Yes — Goody overreached; oral cultures have sophisticated logic in situational form |\n| What is a formula? | Metrically fitting phrase reused across performances (not a cliché — functional unit) |\n| What's the difference between myth and legend? | Myth: sacred, cosmological, set in other time/world; Legend: believed true, set in recent/here |\n| What distinguishes oral history from oral tradition? | Oral history: recorded living memory (methodological); tradition: multi-generational transmission |"
    }
